# /usr/man -- THE MANUAL, all two hundred pages of it, in the dialect
# doc/Manual_Page_Format.md describes.  Each page still lives beside the source it documents
# (cmd/README.md §10) and this is a COPY of it, so an edit to cmd/ls/ls.1.umm is on the disk
# with no install -- the rule /usr/include above is staged by.  The layout is v7's: the
# section DIGIT picks the directory, the subsection letter stays on the FILE, the .umm
# suffix is dropped.  man1/fsck.1m, man3/stdio.3s, man3/exp.3m.
#
# They are listed one by one for /usr/include's reason -- this manifest is kept by hand and
# b6fsutil has no recursive directive -- and the list they must match is B6_STAGE_MAN in the
# top-level CMakeLists.txt.  Comparing the two is a `diff' of this file's source lines
# against that glob, and it is worth running after any page is renamed or deleted, since a
# copy_if_different stages but never removes.
#
# IT DOES NOT CURATE.  The corpus costs about 302 of the 539 free blocks this image had and
# leaves some 237, which is affordable and is half the headroom; a manual with holes in it
# is worth less than the blocks.  SIXTEEN OF THESE PAGES DOCUMENT PROGRAMS THAT ARE NOT ON
# THIS IMAGE -- ac, at, awk, bc, calendar, cron, crypt, dc, expr, m4, mail, make, makekey,
# sa, units and update.  Thirteen are ports cmd/TODO.md still has open and their pages are
# already correct when one lands; mail, ac and sa are in that file's "Not ported, and why"
# table and will never have a program, which is v7's own habit -- a manual page is where a
# decision about a program is recorded whether or not the program exists.
#
# NOTHING READS THEM YET.  There is no man(1) on this disk (cmd/TODO.md C25); a page is
# legible as it stands, which is what the dialect is for.

